                            {
                                "target_word": "twin",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "7b0cad90-54d5-35d2-93ad-843b80c05d36",
                                        "entry_id": "04a6d152-0641-3f0e-bc28-9b3e39943b92",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To separate, divide."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "transitive"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "a191d27c-02aa-3209-ae8e-f54a6761f0ed",
                                        "entry_id": "04a6d152-0641-3f0e-bc28-9b3e39943b92",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To split, part; to go away, depart."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "intransitive"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "c0213ec4-dadb-3dc9-be9a-0bd766e61ebe",
                                        "entry_id": "04a6d152-0641-3f0e-bc28-9b3e39943b92",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To join, unite; to form links between (now especially of two places in different countries); to pair with."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "passive",
                                            "usually"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "7158ee2a-68fe-4e27-adca-bad69b4e891f",
                                                "sense_id": "c0213ec4-dadb-3dc9-be9a-0bd766e61ebe",
                                                "text": "Reading, the English town, is twinned with Clonmel in Ireland."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "cfa6b4f9-88cd-4430-be9e-1e7bbd69bec4",
                                                "sense_id": "c0213ec4-dadb-3dc9-be9a-0bd766e61ebe",
                                                "text": "Coventry twinned with Dresden as an act of peace and reconciliation, both cities having been heavily bombed during the war."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "92f91e51-3f58-488b-8cf8-58069adc5c4a",
                                                "sense_id": "c0213ec4-dadb-3dc9-be9a-0bd766e61ebe",
                                                "text": "Still we moved / Together, twinned, as horse's ear and eye."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "3f440028-1f9a-4ed6-9604-78cde438031d",
                                                "sense_id": "c0213ec4-dadb-3dc9-be9a-0bd766e61ebe",
                                                "text": "Yet, Manet heightens its effect by performing the clever ocular trick of practically twinning her with her pictorial counterpart, Madame Lejosne."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "78f41b78-9585-38e2-9d48-1a3ae9616ae5",
                                        "entry_id": "04a6d152-0641-3f0e-bc28-9b3e39943b92",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To give birth to twins."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "intransitive"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "12cbf7f1-3d7a-4389-a8e3-e71fcfc3fcf2",
                                                "sense_id": "78f41b78-9585-38e2-9d48-1a3ae9616ae5",
                                                "text": "“I’ve run to tell ye,” said the junior shepherd, supporting his exhausted youthful frame against the doorpost, “that you must come directly. Two more ewes have twinned — that’s what’s the matter, Shepherd Oak.”"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "07c0767b-d936-4cf4-a22b-a94614083f74",
                                                "sense_id": "78f41b78-9585-38e2-9d48-1a3ae9616ae5",
                                                "text": "Twinning today is abnormal in all higher primates, and possibly adaptive only in the one species with an obstetrical technology that permits a high rate of survival of twins."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "bb7da4a0-46c7-37c7-83b2-22642380a124",
                                        "entry_id": "04a6d152-0641-3f0e-bc28-9b3e39943b92",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To be, or be like, a twin to (someone else); to match in some way."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "transitive"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "4414b64f-5a5b-4ca3-9455-c436fbf98e50",
                                                "sense_id": "bb7da4a0-46c7-37c7-83b2-22642380a124",
                                                "text": "The invert child and her innocent child are together lost children, twinning each other despite their distinctions."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "d1547437-303e-484a-bdb1-6d6e6717e329",
                                                "sense_id": "bb7da4a0-46c7-37c7-83b2-22642380a124",
                                                "text": "I was awake to the horror of our twinning each other in paralysis, and feared that we would soon talk only about the daily, intimate care serious paralysis demands […]"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "a20dc6e3-fbe6-3e54-a688-26d0c87170b5",
                                        "entry_id": "04a6d152-0641-3f0e-bc28-9b3e39943b92",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To be, or be like, a pair of twins (for example, to dress identically); to be paired or suited."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "intransitive"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "3c96cc3f-5418-4570-b201-7496170d8335",
                                                "sense_id": "a20dc6e3-fbe6-3e54-a688-26d0c87170b5",
                                                "text": "“I see you got us twinning today.” Byrd kissed Lay quickly. “I wanted you to dress like me today,” he admitted."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "ef760a82-8c93-429e-8876-c16d2f373456",
                                                "sense_id": "a20dc6e3-fbe6-3e54-a688-26d0c87170b5",
                                                "text": "“We're totally twinning today!” said Zoey. “You sure are,” said Chef Piper. “I whipped up a special dessert for tonight.”"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
